The Clausius-Clapeyron equation relates the vapor pressure of a liquid to its temperature:

$$\ln P = \frac{\Delta H_{\text{vaporization}}^{\circ}}{RT} + \text{C}$$

where $P$ is the vapor pressure, $\Delta H_{\text{vaporization}}^{\circ}$ is the enthalpy of vaporization, $R$ is the gas constant, and $T$ is the temperature. We can rearrange this equation to solve for the boiling point temperature $T$:

$$T = \frac{\Delta H_{\text{vaporization}}^{\circ}}{R}\left(\exp\left(\frac{R\Delta H_{\text{vaporization}}^{\circ}}{T}\right) - 1\right)$$

We are given $\Delta H_{\text{vaporization}}^{\circ} = 40.656 \mathrm{~kJ} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$ and the normal boiling point is $373.12 \mathrm{~K}$. We need to find the boiling point at an elevation of $8848 \mathrm{~m}$, where the barometric pressure is 253 Torr.

First, we need to convert the pressure to atmospheres (atm) and the elevation to kilometers (km). 1 atm is approximately equal to 101325 Pa, and 1 km is approximately equal to 1000 m. So, 253 Torr is approximately equal to $(253/760) \times 101325$ Pa, and 8848 m is approximately equal to $8848/1000$ km.

Now, we can use the boiling point formula with the given values:

$$T = \frac{40.656 \times 10^3 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}{8.314 \frac{\mathrm{J}}{\mathrm{mol} \cdot \mathrm{K}}}\left(\exp\left(\frac{8.314 \frac{\mathrm{J}}{\mathrm{mol} \cdot \mathrm{K}} \times 40.656 \times 10^3 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}{T}\right) - 1\right)$$

Solving for $T$ gives:

$$T \approx 373.12 \mathrm{~K} \times \frac{253 \times 10^3 \mathrm{Pa}}{101325 \mathrm{Pa}} \times \frac{1 \mathrm{~km}}{8848 \mathrm{~m}} \times \frac{1 \mathrm{~mol}}{40.656 \times 10^3 \mathrm{~J}} \approx 355 \mathrm{~K}$$

So the boiling point for water on the top of Mt. Everest is approximately 355 K, which corresponds to option (A).
